{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/2026-07-14-moominvalley-tove-jansson-improved-b1/audio/main-1da535d459a3.mp3","durationSec":370.584,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":6.16,"text":"Tove Jansson was born in Helsinki, Finland, in 1914, and she died in 2001."},{"start":7.28,"end":12.34,"text":"Her life covered almost the whole twentieth century, with all its wars and changes."},{"start":13.22,"end":15.1,"text":"She grew up in an artistic family."},{"start":15.98,"end":23.5,"text":"Her mother was an illustrator and her father was a sculptor, so drawing and making things were normal, everyday activities in her home."},{"start":24.58,"end":26.24,"text":"Tove was a Swedish-speaking Finn."},{"start":27.02,"end":37.22,"text":"This is important: Finland has two national languages, Finnish and Swedish, and Tove belonged to the smaller Swedish-speaking minority."},{"start":38.36,"end":41.66,"text":"She wrote all of her books in Swedish, not in Finnish."},{"start":42.46,"end":49.82,"text":"In a way, she was already a kind of outsider in her own country, and this feeling appears again and again in her stories."},{"start":51.1,"end":56.24,"text":"Before she became famous as an author, Tove was a serious painter and illustrator."},{"start":56.24,"end":61.88,"text":"She studied art in Helsinki, Stockholm, and Paris, and she painted for her whole life."},{"start":62.52,"end":64.72,"text":"She often said that painting was her real work."},{"start":65.36,"end":72.64,"text":"She also drew political cartoons during the Second World War for a magazine called Garm, where she made fun of dictators like Hitler and Stalin."},{"start":73.58,"end":79.3,"text":"So when she started writing about small, round creatures in a peaceful valley, she was not a naive young woman."},{"start":80.08,"end":85.78,"text":"She was an adult artist who had seen war, fear, and hate, and who wanted to imagine a kinder world."},{"start":86.7,"end":94.02,"text":"The first Moomin book, called The Moomins and the Great Flood, was published in 1945, just after the Second World War ended."},{"start":94.6,"end":95.9,"text":"This is an important detail."},{"start":96.82,"end":101.04,"text":"Europe was tired, broken, and full of families who had lost their homes."},{"start":101.6,"end":110.78,"text":"In this small book, a mother Moomin and her child are searching for the missing father, walking through a strange and frightening world of dark forests and rising water."},{"start":111.6,"end":118.76,"text":"It is a story about being lost and looking for family — exactly what many real people felt in 1945."},{"start":120.72,"end":124.64,"text":"The next book, Comet in Moominland, came out in 1946."},{"start":125.82,"end":132.42,"text":"In this story, a huge comet is coming to destroy the world, and the Moomins must travel to find out if they can survive."},{"start":133.4,"end":142.96,"text":"Again, the story is gentle, but the fear behind it is real: children in 1946 knew what it was like to hear that the world could end."},{"start":144.12,"end":152.54,"text":"Then, in 1948, Jansson published Finn Family Moomintroll, which is often the first Moomin book that readers meet today."},{"start":153.5,"end":157.98,"text":"This one is warmer, funnier, and full of magic hats and small adventures."},{"start":158.58,"end":170.46,"text":"Together, these three early books built the world of Moominvalley: a place where scary things happen, but where a family and their friends always come home to a lamp in the window and warm soup on the table."},{"start":171.48,"end":177.08,"text":"The Moomin stories are famous for their characters, and almost all of them are, in some way, outsiders."},{"start":178.3,"end":184.56,"text":"At the center is Moomintroll, a young Moomin who is curious, sensitive, and sometimes afraid."},{"start":185.56,"end":191.44,"text":"He asks the questions that many children and shy adults are afraid to ask: Am I loved?"},{"start":192.06,"end":193.26,"text":"Will my friend come back?"},{"start":193.92,"end":195.56,"text":"What happens if everything changes?"},{"start":196.6,"end":199.96,"text":"His mother, Moominmamma, is calm and warm."},{"start":200.86,"end":205.04,"text":"She carries a handbag full of useful things and she always has time to listen."},{"start":205.84,"end":210.58,"text":"For many readers, she is one of the kindest mothers in all of children's literature."},{"start":210.58,"end":213.16,"text":"Moominpappa is more dramatic."},{"start":213.64,"end":218.64,"text":"He wears a tall black hat, writes his memoirs, and dreams of adventures on the sea."},{"start":219.36,"end":221.34,"text":"He can be silly, but he loves his family."},{"start":222.52,"end":224.18,"text":"Around this family come the visitors."},{"start":224.18,"end":230.96,"text":"Snufkin is a quiet, free spirit who travels alone with a green hat and a small tent."},{"start":231.86,"end":239.06,"text":"He leaves every autumn and returns every spring, and Moomintroll waits for him like we wait for a best friend."},{"start":239.84,"end":242.36,"text":"Little My is small, sharp, and fierce."},{"start":242.94,"end":246.66,"text":"She says exactly what she thinks and is never polite just to be nice."},{"start":247.6,"end":259.1,"text":"There are also stranger creatures: the lonely Groke, who freezes the ground where she sits because no one has ever loved her, and the shy Hattifatteners, who travel in silent groups toward the horizon."},{"start":260.1,"end":269.04,"text":"Together, these characters show a simple, powerful idea: a good community is not one where everyone is the same, but one where very different people are welcome."},{"start":270.32,"end":272.44,"text":"The Moomins did not stay only in books."},{"start":273.36,"end":280.84,"text":"From 1954, Tove Jansson drew a Moomin comic strip for The Evening News, a big newspaper in London."},{"start":281.5,"end":285.12,"text":"The strip appeared every day and reached millions of readers around the world."},{"start":285.98,"end":290.04,"text":"This was hard work, and later her brother Lars took over most of the drawing."},{"start":290.6,"end":297.36,"text":"Because of this comic strip, the Moomins became truly international, known far outside the Nordic countries."},{"start":298.36,"end":299.86,"text":"Tove Jansson also wrote for adults."},{"start":300.9,"end":311.36,"text":"In 1972, she published The Summer Book, a quiet novel about an old woman and her small granddaughter spending summers together on a tiny island."},{"start":312.1,"end":321.88,"text":"There are no Moomins in it, but the same feeling is there: small moments, honest feelings, and love between people who are very different in age."},{"start":322.94,"end":330.36,"text":"The Moomins arrived in Japan in a very big way in 1990, when the anime Tanoshii Moomin Ikka was shown on television."},{"start":331.24,"end":338.02,"text":"This series was beautiful, calm, and faithful to Jansson's world, and it became extremely popular."},{"start":338.82,"end":343.58,"text":"For many Japanese fans, this anime is their first and deepest memory of the Moomins."},{"start":344.08,"end":351.42,"text":"In 2019, Moominvalley Park opened in Hanno, in Saitama prefecture, near Tokyo."},{"start":352.3,"end":359.78,"text":"It is the only Moomin theme park in the world outside Finland, and many Japanese learners of English have already visited it."},{"start":360.32,"end":370.1,"text":"Standing in that park, next to a wooden Moomin House by a lake, you can feel how far a small Swedish-language book from 1945 has traveled."}],"words":[{"text":"Tove","start":0,"end":0.28},{"text":"Jansson","start":0.28,"end":0.68},{"text":"was","start":0.68,"end":1.06},{"text":"born","start":1.06,"end":1.34},{"text":"in","start":1.34,"end":1.54},{"text":"Helsinki","start":1.54,"end":1.9},{"text":"Finland","start":1.9,"end":2.44},{"text":"in","start":2.44,"end":2.94},{"text":"1914","start":2.94,"end":3.7},{"text":"and","start":4.54,"e